<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20680] socklnd: fix delayed receive state publication race</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20680</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Problem&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two ARM64 GB300 clients running Lustre 2.15.90_OCI_v5 hit a SockLND scheduler LBUG while receiving delayed LNet PUT payloads:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LASSERT(conn-&amp;gt;ksnc_rx_nkiov &amp;gt; 0)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the failure, the connection was live and in SOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD. Subsequent kdump memory showed a valid receive vector with ksnc_rx_nkiov = 1, which rules out a malformed or zero-length payload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Root cause&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ksocknal_process_receive() calls lnet_parse(), which can defer the receive and invoke ksocknal_recv() asynchronously. The callback initializes the receive tuple (message, lengths, iov/kiov pointers, and nkiov), then writes ksnc_rx_state = SOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD while holding kss_lock.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scheduler reads ksnc_rx_state without that lock. On weakly ordered ARM64, it can observe SOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD while still observing stale receive-tuple data, including ksnc_rx_nkiov = 0. It then reaches ksocknal_recv_kiov() and triggers the assertion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Proposed fix&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Publish SOCKNAL_RX_LNET_PAYLOAD with smp_store_release() after the receive tuple is initialized. In ksocknal_process_receive(), consume the state with smp_load_acquire() and use that acquired value for the state check. This guarantees that observing RX_LNET_PAYLOAD also makes the preceding tuple initialization visible.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
